## WebGL 1 for the page's canvas, written in JavaScript over the engine's
## built-ins and run once per page after webapi.ail (it wraps the canvas
## `getContext` defined there). A context keeps all of its state, validates
## calls and sets `getError` as the WebGL 1.0 specification says, parses
## shader sources (compile and link status, active attributes and uniforms,
## uniform values) and holds buffers, textures, renderbuffers, framebuffers
## and vertex array objects. It rasterizes nothing but `clear` into the
## drawing buffer (`readPixels` sees it): draw calls validate, then do
## nothing, and the window never shows a WebGL canvas. Parameters and the
## extension list follow Chromium's (the same names in the same order);
## the unmasked vendor and renderer say what this is: "Garlic". `webgl2`
## adds the 3.00 API on the same state: GLSL ES 3.00 shader parsing (layout
## locations, uniform blocks, integer and unsigned types), vertex array
## objects, samplers, queries, sync objects, transform feedback objects,
## 3D and array textures, immutable storage, indexed buffer bindings,
## separate read and draw framebuffers, `drawBuffers` and `clearBuffer*`.
